  "season": "autumn",
  "minutes": 20,
  "title": {
    "en": "Dunlin, stints and small sandpipers"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Dunlin, Little and Temminck's Stint, Curlew Sandpiper, Sanderling and Pectoral Sandpiper"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "You know a Dunlin from a Little Stint. Now we put the whole small-wader family in order, and look at the plumages you really meet. In spring the adults are in breeding colours and most of the birds pass through in a hurry in May. In autumn, from late July, the adults return in worn plumage and the juveniles follow in August and September, and the juveniles are the key: fresh, neat and easy to separate.\n\nThe method is the same for every small wader: size and shape first (bill length and curve, leg colour), then the pattern on the back, then the head, then the voice. A Little Stint in September has a pale V on the back and a split eyebrow. A Curlew Sandpiper is taller, with a long curved bill and peachy breast. A Pectoral Sandpiper stands upright, with a sharp line between its streaked breast and white belly.\n\nWhere to look: muddy edges of reservoirs and pits, brackish pools, and estuaries at high tide. The Dunlin is the standard. Everything else is compared with it."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "mantle V"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale V on the back of a juvenile Little Stint or Sanderling, made by pale edges on the back feathers."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "supercilium"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The pale stripe above the eye. On Little Stint it is often split in two in front of the eye."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "breast band"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A band of streaks across the breast, which ends sharply against the white belly, as in the Pectoral Sandpiper."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "hind toe"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The small toe at the back of the foot. Sanderling has none, which is why it runs so smoothly."
      }
    }
  ],
